## 3. Recovery and Corruption Testing

### 3.1 Purpose

Recovery testing was used to evaluate how the improved recovery system performed when the encoded zero-width payload was intentionally subjected to controlled corruption.

### 3.2 Corruption Scenarios

The testing included controlled scenarios involving:

* Zero-width character removal
* Zero-width character alteration
* Mixed removal and alteration

In particular the following levels of corruption were applied:
| Corruption Type | Corruption Levels Tested |
|---|---|
| Removal | 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60% |
| Alteration | 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50% |
| Mixed Removal + Alteration | Approximately 20%, 30%, 40% |

### 3.3 Recovery Testing Procedure

The general recovery-testing procedure was:

1. Encode a known plaintext message.
2. Apply a controlled corruption scenario to the encoded payload.
3. Pass the corrupted payload to the recovery/decoder system.
4. Record the number of characters remaining and/or altered.
5. Attempt to recover the original hidden message.
6. Record whether decoding succeeded or failed.
7. Compare the recovered message with the original plaintext.
8. Record the results and any decoder error messages.

### 3.4 Recovery Testing Measurements

Recovery tests recorded measurements including:

* Original message
* Type of corruption
* Target corruption and Actual corruption percentages
* Original encoded payload size
* Number of characters remaining
* Number of characters removed
* Number of characters altered
* Survival rate
* Decode success or failure
* Recovered message
* Whether the recovered message matched the original
* Decoder error information when recovery failed

---

## 4. Testing Metrics

### 4.1 Survival Rate

For this project, survival rate refers to the percentage of encoded zero-width characters that remained present after a test or corruption scenario.

Survival rate was calculated as:

$$
\text{Survival Rate} =
\frac{\text{Number of Zero-Width Characters Remaining}}
{\text{Original Number of Zero-Width Characters}}
\times 100
$$

[Important clarification: survival rate does not necessarily indicate successful message recovery.]

### 4.2 Decode Success

Decode success was treated as a separate measurement from character survival. A test was considered successfully recovered when the decoder was able to reconstruct the hidden message and the recovered message matched the original plaintext.

### 4.3 Controlled Corruption Results

The recovery results represent performance under the specific controlled corruption scenarios tested. They should not be interpreted as a universal maximum corruption tolerance for arbitrary real-world corruption or platform transformations.

